{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C281", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1994 five-franc coin from France. The side shown has a large 5 and FRANCS above a flowering branch, with the national motto Liberté, Égalité, Fraternité around the rim and the date below.", "new": "A 1994 French 5-franc coin, one of the last of its kind. The franc was France's money for over 600 years, and this coin carries La Semeuse — the Sower — a woman scattering seeds into the wind, drawn by Oscar Roty back in 1897. She first appeared on French silver coins, and when France switched the 5 francs to base metal in 1970, she came along for the ride. This 1994 piece was struck in Paris just eight years before the franc gave way to the euro in 2002. The reverse shows the value above a flowering branch, ringed by the national motto: Liberté, Égalité, Fraternité.", "source": "coinvarieties.com France 1993 5 francs entry (KM#930, La Semeuse by Oscar Roty, nickel-plated copper-nickel 10g 29mm, Paris mint 1970-2001); Numiscorner 5 francs 1994 listing", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C282", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1965 fifty-para coin from Yugoslavia. The side shown has a large 50 in a beaded circle, with the word para in the country's languages and Cyrillic and Latin letters around it, and the date split as 19 and 65.", "new": "A 1965 Yugoslav 50-para coin, struck the year the 'hard dinar' was born. In 1965 Yugoslavia revalued its currency, chopping two zeros off the old dinar, and this was one of the first coins of the new system. The word 'para' appears in four languages around the value — Serbian Cyrillic, Croatian, Slovene, and Macedonian — because Yugoslavia was six republics stitched into one federation. The other side carries the socialist coat of arms: six torches for six republics, ringed by the date 29 November 1943, the day Tito's partisans declared their new Yugoslavia. KM#46, struck in brass at Belgrade.", "source": "Numista/Colnect Yugoslavia 50 para KM#46.2 (1965-1981 4th series, Belgrade, brass, 25.5mm); heritcoin KM#46 1965 entry; Foronum 50 para KM#561", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C279", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1997 one-piso coin from the Philippines. The side shown has José Rizal, the national hero, with the words Republika ng Pilipinas (Republic of the Philippines), a large 1, PISO and the date.", "new": "A 1997 Philippine 1-piso coin. The portrait is José Rizal, the writer and doctor whose execution by the Spanish in 1896 lit the fuse for Philippine independence — the country's national hero, on its most-used coin. This is from the 'New Design' series that ran from 1995 to 2003, when the Philippines was rebuilding its coinage after years of high inflation had made the old centavo coins nearly worthless. The reverse (not shown) carries the value and the Central Bank seal. Struck in nickel-plated steel.", "source": "Standard references: Philippines 1 piso 1995-2003 New Design series, Jose Rizal obverse, nickel-plated steel (Numista N# series)", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C280", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2007 twenty-five-cent coin from Belize. The side shown has a large 25 and CENTS inside a beaded circle, with BELIZE above and the date below. The other side, not photographed yet, carries Queen Elizabeth II.", "new": "A 2007 Belizean 25-cent coin. Belize is a small English-speaking country on the Caribbean coast of Central America — it was British Honduras until 1981 — and its coins still carry the British monarch. This one shows Queen Elizabeth II on the obverse. The reverse (shown) has a big 25 inside a beaded circle, the workhorse quarter of Belizean pocket change. Belize kept its own dollar, pegged two-to-one against the US dollar since 1978, so this 25 cents has always been worth about twelve and a half US cents.", "source": "Standard references: Belize 25 cents 2007, Elizabeth II obverse, beaded-circle reverse; BZD pegged 2:1 to USD since 1978", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C276", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2015 one-cent coin from the Bahamas, the first Bahamian coin in the collection. The side shown carries the words COMMONWEALTH OF THE BAHAMAS and the date. The coin is heavily corroded, with green patches across the surface.", "new": "A 2015 Bahamian 1-cent coin, the first Bahamian coin in the collection. The Bahamas went independent from Britain in 1973, but kept the Queen on its coins for decades — this one is from the modern series with the national coat of arms on the front. Look close at the shield: a Santa Maria ship for Columbus's 1492 landing, a marlin and a flamingo holding it up. The back shows three starfish — the 1-cent reverse. Struck in copper-plated steel, it circulated in Nassau's straw markets and fish fries, worth about one US cent.", "source": "Owner photo (2026-10-04): Commonwealth of the Bahamas coat of arms obverse, three starfish 1 cent reverse; copper-plated steel", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C277", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2004 ten-cent coin from Bermuda. The side shown has the Bermuda Easter lily, with the date above it and TEN CENTS below. The other side, not photographed yet, carries Queen Elizabeth II.", "new": "A 2004 Bermudian 10-cent coin. Bermuda is a British island territory in the Atlantic, famous for its pink-sand beaches — and for the Bermuda Easter lily, the flower on this coin's reverse. The lily became Bermuda's signature export in the 1800s, when island farmers shipped bulbs all over the world for Easter. The obverse carries Queen Elizabeth II in her tiara, facing right. Bermuda's dollar has been pegged one-to-one with the US dollar for decades, so this dime-sized 10 cents spent just like an American dime.", "source": "Owner photo (2026-10-04): Bermuda Easter lily reverse, Elizabeth II tiara obverse, copper-nickel; BMD pegged 1:1 to USD", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C274", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1995 Mexican 10 centavos from the nuevo peso reform series struck 1992-2009. The reverse shows a large 10¢ with the date above and the Mexico City mint mark below, framed by a border of points and beads taken from the Aztec Calendar Stone's Ring of Sacrifice. These stainless steel centavos were struck for everyday small change in the years after Mexico revalued the peso.", "new": "A 1995 Mexican 10-centavos coin, from the 'nuevo peso' series. In 1993 Mexico knocked three zeros off its peso — 1,000 old pesos became 1 new peso — because inflation had made the old money absurd. This little coin is from the first years of that reform. The obverse has the Mexican golden eagle on a cactus with a snake in its beak, the legend of the founding of Tenochtitlan. The reverse shows the denomination ringed by stylized rays from the Aztec Sun Stone, with the little 'Mo' mint mark for Mexico City — the oldest mint in the Americas, running since 1535.", "source": "Owner chat photo (2026-10-02): nuevo peso 10 centavos 1992-2009 series, Mo mint mark Mexico City; Aztec Sun Stone rays reverse", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C275", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A British five pence dated 1992, struck in cupronickel for everyday circulation. The obverse shows Raphael Maklouf's crowned portrait of Elizabeth II with the date in the legend. The reverse carries the crowned thistle design marked FIVE PENCE. The Royal Mint at Llantrisant struck about 453 million five pence coins in 1992.", "new": "A 1992 British 5 pence, the year the denomination got its big shrink. Before 1990 the 5p was a chunky coin the size of a shilling; in 1990 Britain cut it nearly in half, and this is the new small kind. The obverse shows Raphael Maklouf's portrait of a young Queen Elizabeth II wearing the royal crown. The reverse has a crowned thistle — the emblem of Scotland. Struck in cupronickel at the Royal Mint in Llantrisant, Wales, it's still legal tender today. The 1992 mintage ran over 450 million.", "source": "Owner chat photo (2026-10-04): UK 5p small type from 1990, Maklouf portrait, crowned thistle reverse, Royal Mint Llantrisant; KM#937b", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C186", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2002 5 cents from Cayman Islands. The obverse shows Rank-Broadley Elizabeth II; the reverse shows shrimp; CAYMAN ISLANDS, ELIZABETH II, date. Struck in nickel-plated steel.", "new": "A 2002 Cayman Islands 5-cent coin. The Caymans are three tiny British islands in the Caribbean, famous for offshore banking and for some of the prettiest diving water on earth. The obverse shows Queen Elizabeth II; the reverse shows a shrimp — a nod to the islands' fishing heritage. This is the second Cayman year in the collection, after the 1996 twins. Struck in nickel-plated steel, KM#132, with a mintage of about 2.5 million — small change from a small place.", "source": "Collection record: Cayman Islands 5 cents 2002, Rank-Broadley Elizabeth II, shrimp reverse, nickel-plated steel, KM#132, mintage ~2.5M", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C203", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2001 1 rupee from India. Value side रुपया, big 1, RUPEE, 2001, Noida dot, wheat ears flanking, Ashoka Lion Capital reverse. Struck in stainless steel.", "new": "A 2001 Indian 1-rupee coin, the everyday workhorse of the world's largest democracy. The value side says रुपया — 'rupaya' — in Hindi script, with a big 1 flanked by wheat ears, and the little dot that marks the Noida mint near Delhi. The other side carries the Ashoka Lion Capital, India's national emblem, from a 3rd-century-BC stone pillar. India struck billions of these in stainless steel — this one bought chai, bus fares, and market change. The rupee itself is one of the oldest currency names still in use, going back to 16th-century India.", "source": "Collection record + photo: India 1 rupee 2001, Noida dot mint mark, Ashoka Lion Capital, stainless steel, KM#92.2", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C278", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1980 Swiss 20-rappen coin. The side shown has the head of Libertas, the figure of liberty, with the words CONFOEDERATIO HELVETICA (Swiss Confederation) and the date.", "new": "A 1980 Swiss 20-rappen coin. Switzerland's coinage barely changes across the decades — the head of Libertas, the figure of liberty, has looked out from this denomination since the 1800s, with CONFOEDERATIO HELVETICA around her. The reverse holds the big 20 in a wreath. This one was first misfiled as a 1930 2-franc piece; the photo and the owner set it straight — the smaller size and the wreath give it away. Struck in copper-nickel, engraved by Karl Schwenzer and Carl Friedrich Voigt. Swiss coins stay in circulation for generations — this one may still buy a tram ticket in Zurich.", "source": "Owner photo (2026-10-04) + owner correction: Switzerland 20 rappen 1980, Libertas/Confoederatio Helvetica, KM#29a, Schwenzer/Voigt", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C283", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2002 ten-cent euro coin from Germany, from the first year of the euro. The German side shows the Brandenburg Gate in Berlin inside a ring of twelve stars, with the date and the D of the Munich mint below.", "new": "A 2002 German 10-euro-cent coin, from the very first year of euro cash. On January 1, 2002, twelve countries swapped their old money for the euro, and Germany's national side shows the Brandenburg Gate in Berlin — the symbol of German reunification, drawn by Reinhart Heinsdorff. Around it, twelve stars for twelve founding euro members. The common side (not shown) has the map of Europe by Luc Luycx. The little 'D' is the Munich mint. Struck in Nordic gold, KM#210 — pocket change from the biggest currency changeover in history.", "source": "Owner photo (2026-10-06): Germany 10 euro cent 2002, Brandenburg Gate national side (Heinsdorff), 12 EU stars, D Munich mint, Nordic gold, KM#210", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C010", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1996 25 cents from Netherlands. The obverse shows Beatrix portrait; the reverse shows squared grid field with large 25; ct, date. Struck in nickel.", "new": "A 1996 Dutch 25-cent coin — a kwartje, from the last generation of the guilder. The obverse shows Queen Beatrix; the reverse is the famous Dutch grid design with a big 25. For over 300 years the Netherlands used guilders, and the kwartje was the coin everyone knew — phone booths, parking meters, and coffee machines ran on them. When the euro arrived in 2002, the guilder was demonetized, and billions of kwartjes went back to the bank. Struck in nickel at Utrecht (the caduceus mint mark), KM#204, mintage about 24.8 million.", "source": "Collection record: Netherlands 25 cents 1996, Beatrix obverse, grid reverse, Utrecht caduceus, nickel, KM#204; guilder demonetized 2002-02-28", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C004", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2000 Dutch 5-euro-cent coin. The Dutch side shows a portrait of Queen Beatrix with the words BEATRIX KONINGIN DER NEDERLANDEN (Beatrix, Queen of the Netherlands). The other side, shared by every euro country, was designed by Luc Luycx. Struck in copper-plated steel.", "new": "A 2000 Dutch 5-euro-cent coin, struck two years before euro cash even existed. The Netherlands minted euro coins dated 1999, 2000, and 2001 ahead of the 2002 changeover and stockpiled them in vaults. The national side shows Queen Beatrix with BEATRIX KONINGIN DER NEDERLANDEN around her, plus twelve EU stars. The common globe side is by Luc Luycx. Struck in copper-plated steel at Utrecht, KM#236, mintage over 184 million — small copper small change from the country that ran the world's first stock exchange.", "source": "Collection record: Netherlands 5 euro cent 2000, Beatrix portrait, 12 stars, copper-plated steel, Utrecht, KM#236", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:30:28Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C142", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2000 Dutch 1-euro-cent coin. The Dutch side shows a portrait of Queen Beatrix with the words BEATRIX KONINGIN DER NEDERLANDEN (Beatrix, Queen of the Netherlands). The other side, shared by every euro country, was designed by Luc Luycx. Struck in copper-plated steel.", "new": "A 2000 Dutch 1-euro-cent coin — the smallest of the euro family. Like its 5-cent sibling, it was minted ahead of the 2002 euro launch and shows Queen Beatrix on the national side. At 16.25 mm it's one of the tiniest coins in the collection, struck in copper-plated steel. The Dutch 1-cent was controversial from day one: the Netherlands (like Finland) quickly stopped using 1- and 2-cent coins in cash, rounding everything to the nearest 5 cents — so these mostly went straight from the mint roll into jars. KM#234, mintage about 276.8 million, Utrecht mint.", "source": "Collection record: Netherlands 1 euro cent 2000, Beatrix portrait, 16.25mm, copper-plated steel, Utrecht, KM#234; NL rounds cash to 5c", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "SELF-DIRECTED@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-improvement", "inputs": []}}
